 Each number is right-aligned in a 7-character column followed by a space.
+
+With `--json` (which may appear anywhere among the arguments), tally instead
+prints exactly one JSON document followed by a newline:
+
+```
+$ node bin/tally.js --json notes.txt todo.txt
+{"files":[{"path":"notes.txt","lines":3,"words":12,"chars":80},{"path":"todo.txt","lines":1,"words":4,"chars":19}],"total":{"lines":4,"words":16,"chars":99}}
+```
+
+`files` has one entry per successfully read file, in command-line order;
+`total` sums their counts and is always present, even for a single file or
+no files at all (`{"files":[],"total":{"lines":0,"words":0,"chars":0}}`).
+
 A file that cannot be read is reported on stderr as `tally: cannot read <path>`,
-the other files are still counted, and the exit code is 1. An unknown option
-prints a usage line on stderr and exits with code 2. Otherwise the exit code is 0.
+the other files are still counted (in JSON mode it is left out of `files`), and
+the exit code is 1. An unknown option prints a usage line on stderr and exits
+with code 2. Otherwise the exit code is 0.
 
